Expense Sharing Backend:

-> About the Project
    This project is a backend system for an expense sharing application (similar to Splitwise).
    It allows users to create groups, add shared expenses, and see who owes money and who should receive money.

    The main goal of this assignment was to focus on backend design, API structure, and business logic, so no frontend is implemented.

-> Tech Stack Used
   Java
   Spring Boot
   Spring Data JPA
   PostgreSQL (for persistent storage)
   Docker
   Deployed on Render

-> What This Backend Can Do
   Create users
   Create groups and add users to groups
   Add shared expenses within a group
   Support different expense split types
   Equal split
   Exact amount split
   Percentage split
   Calculate balances for each user in a group

-> How the System Works (High Level)
   Each expense belongs to a group
   One user pays the expense
   The expense is split among group members based on the chosen split type
   Balances are calculated by:
   Adding the full amount to the payer
   Subtracting the owed amount from each participant
   A positive balance means the user should receive money.
   A negative balance means the user owes money.

-> API Overview
   1)Users
    POST /users – create a user
    GET /users – get all users

   2)Groups
    POST /groups – create a group Example: POST -> https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups?name=groupName
    POST /groups/{groupId}/addUser/{userId} – add user to group Example: POST -> https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups/1/addUser/104
    GET /groups/{groupId} - to check users in the group with ID 
    GET /groups/{groupId}/balances – get balances for the group Example: GET -> https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups/1/balances

  Note: All APIs can be tested using Postman or any REST client.

-> Deployment
   The application is containerized using Docker and deployed on Render.
   PostgreSQL is used as the database to make sure data is not lost when the application restarts.
   Database credentials are managed using environment variables.
